clearly much worse. in still.json (216 calibration) the computed positions are:
20.031287 sec: [-0.1728, -1.6061, -0.5360] = 1.7019 m = 0.0849 m / s
93.116040 sec: [0.08543, -22.132, -4.8844] = 22.664 m = 0.2434 m / s
in still20.json (20-sec calibration) the overall drift rate is 138.3614 / 216.336 = 0.639 m / s. at 20.000248 sec the
computed position is [-0.042, -0.232, -0.0329] = 0.238 m = 0.0119 m / s drift

so current status is that it appears (n=1) to ~work while moving, but it clearly significantly drifts while standing
still, with the drift being significantly worse on longer periods; gyro is not perfect but has not been shown to be
distinguishable from perfect under real-world use.

i wonder if perhaps the accelerometer's response to gravity is not fully consistent. should check arbitrary n-second
blocks in rec_20260828_182912 and see if they are meaningfully different. the android api documentation has a code
snippet using a low-pass filter to isolate gravity.

