and now K = 0.982... and the model is back to just spitting back whatever the sensor says.

it has previously occurred to me that this filtering system can't fix the drift during the calibration phase because I'm
feeding it a prior (i.e. Q) that the device is getting pushed around, so it expects pushes and believes the sensor, when
in fact there are no pushes. I think this could be 'fixed' (in a somewhat degenerate sense) by just asserting Q = 0
which i think leads to K[k] = 0 for all k. going to try it just to confirm. sigma2_j = 0, only running for calibration
period:
    [nibawr@amaranth py]$ ./scratch ../../pulled/rec_20260708_205242.csv 20 > slide-kalman4-y.json
    [...]
    sigma2_j / sigma2_z  = 0.000000
